# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One repository, two trees: the docs at the top level and the Phase 0/1 spike
# under spike/. The spike used to be its own git repository and was ignored
# here, which meant the entire codebase was invisible to the repo that carried
# all the reasoning about it. It is imported as one squashed commit; its 14
# original commits are kept only locally, because their history carries the
# fixture blobs excluded below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Not published, and the reason is not size ----------------------------
# The real captures are screenshots of real posts by identifiable people, and
# the rendered cards contain the same content. Publishing them would put other
# people's handles, faces and words in a public repo under this project's name,
# which is the one thing the design's "no attribution" choice must not be used
# to do. They stay on the machine that took them.
#
# Consequence, stated because it is a real cost: a stranger cannot re-derive
# the measured device numbers in spike/results/, and cannot run quite every
# check either. tools/chunks.test.mjs reads one of these captures by name and
# skips seven checks without it (it says so, and counts them); make-fixture.mjs
# writes IHDR/IDAT/IEND only, so it is not a substitute for a capture carrying
# an ICC profile. 516 of 526 checks run in a clone. An earlier version of this
# comment claimed all of them did.
spike/fixtures/screenshots/
spike/results/cards/

# Fetched HTML used once to measure the oEmbed / Open Graph responses.
# measured-endpoints.md is the artifact; these are the raw bytes it came from.
fixtures/pages/
fixtures/og-pages.tar.gz

# --- Signing material. Never commit any of it. ----------------------------
# The release keystore is what lets a recipient take an update: Android refuses
# an update signed by a different key. Losing it is bad; publishing it is worse,
# because anyone could then sign an APK that installs over this one.
*.jks
*.keystore
*.p12
*.pfx
*.p8
*.key
*.pem
*.mobileprovision

# Every filename this project could plausibly put a signing password in. The
# list is long on purpose: React Native's own signing guide -- the link quoted
# in plugins/withReleaseSigning.js -- tells you to put
# MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_PASSWORD into gradle.properties, and a path-scoped
# `git add spike/` would have committed that in cleartext to a public repo.
# A review found every one of these unignored.
keystore.properties
keystore.properties.*
signing.properties
gradle.properties
local.properties
passwords.txt
twitwa-release
.env
.env.*

# --- Build output and dependencies ----------------------------------------
node_modules/
spike/.expo/
spike/dist/
spike/web-build/
spike/expo-env.d.ts
spike/android/
spike/ios/
spike/.kotlin/
spike/.metro-health-check*
*.orig.*

# --- Scratch written by tools/drive-pick.sh on each run -------------------
spike/tools/ui.xml
spike/tools/hit.txt
spike/tools/hit.err

# --- Local env, editor, OS ------------------------------------------------
.env*.local
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
